<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-07">
    <title>Week 7</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-energy-finish">
        <title>Chapter 5.3.2-5.3.5, 5.4, 5.8-5.11</title>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 10 Covers 5.3.1 through 5.4</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 5.3.2 and 5.3.3 extend the work energy principle to 3D <em>and</em>
                        to multi-particle systems simultaneously, but this is done with some sleight
                        of hand. The first thing to note here is the appearance of the dot product: the
                        work only depends on the projection of the force along the path of the particle.
                        This is important!
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The language of <term>line integrals</term> used in the text is likely to be
                        intimidating. I will not ask you to evaluate line integrals, but the language
                        will be important. All that equation (5.11) asks you to do is add up the little
                        bit of work done by the external forces at different points in the path. Examples
                        5.9 and 5.10 show you how this is done in practice, which you will learn in detail
                        in Calculus 3.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        It is very important that you realize that equation (5.15) does not represent the total
                        work done on a system of particles. It is, in fact, nothing more than a mathematical trick, but 
                        it is useful! What equation (5.15) is telling you is how much of the external work done on the
                        system goes into changing the motion of the center of mass. In many problems, this is all you need
                        to know. In other problems (e.g thermodynamics) this is useless.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Long-story short: the actual work-energy principle is the one we discussed in class. The total
                        work done, which is the some of the work done by the individual forces, using the displacement
                        <em>of the point of application of each individual force</em>, changes the total energy of the
                        system, which can have many forms. Phew!
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 5.4 deals with the subtleties of finding the potential energy attached to a force
                        in 3D. It turns out that not every vector function <m>\vec{F}(\vec{r})</m> can be derived
                        from a potential <m>U(\vec{r})</m>. The notes at the end of chapter 5 have a lot to say about
                        this, and you'll discuss this in detail in Calc 3 and beyond.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The actual point that section 5.8 is making is this: often, when you're dealing with
                        non-conservative forces like friction, it's conceptually cleaner to just think about
                        the motion of the center of mass instead of worrying about thermal energy.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 5.9-5.11 are fun, but optional.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>